The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
end_requests(fc, &fpq->processing);
fuse_abort_conn(fc, false);
```
Reason:
The `fuse_dev_release()` function assumes exclusive access to the `fpq->processing` list when calling `end_requests()`, but `fuse_abort_conn()` can also access this list concurrently without any synchronization. This can lead to race conditions and data corruption if both functions attempt to modify the list at the same time.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
spin_lock(&fpq->lock);
list_splice_init(&fpq->processing, &to_end);
spin_unlock(&fpq->lock);
```
Reason:
The patch introduces a spin lock around the access to the `fpq->processing` list, ensuring that only one function can modify the list at a time. This prevents concurrent access from `fuse_abort_conn()` and `fuse_dev_release()`, thus eliminating the race condition and ensuring data integrity.

